---
import PageHead from '../components/PageHead.astro';
import { CORE_COMMIT_SHORT, CORE_REPO, roadmapSnapshot, specs, versions } from '../data/facts';
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';

const snapshotUrl = `${CORE_REPO}/commit/${roadmapSnapshot.commit}`;
const snapshotShort = roadmapSnapshot.commit.slice(0, 7);
---

<Base
  title="Vision & Roadmap"
  description="RoboLang's research direction, its development status on 28 October 2026, the Gate 2 interoperability sequence and the planned work beyond it. Nothing on this page runs on a robot."
>
  <PageHead slot="head" index="05" label="Vision & Roadmap" title="Vision & roadmap">
    Where RoboLang is trying to go, and how that differs from what you can install today.
  </PageHead>

  <div class="prose">
    <div class="note">
      <p>
        <strong>Plans and features are marked apart.</strong> Stages marked Delivered or Simulation only
        are in the core repository today. Nothing on this page runs on a robot, enforces a safety
        limit or includes an AI model, and plans may change.
      </p>
    </div>

    <h2>Vision</h2>
    <p>
      The research question behind RoboLang is whether one small, precisely specified task language
      can sit between the people and AI systems that decide what a robot should do and the varied
      software that makes robots move.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Interoperable tasks.</strong> The same task description, checked once, understood by
        different robots through adapters that declare what each robot can do.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Multi-robot communication.</strong> Structured, versioned messages for handing work
        from one robot to another, in place of free text.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>AI-generated programs.</strong> A language strict enough that a task written by a
        language model can be rejected with a precise reason before it reaches anything physical.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      The project builds toward this in order: a correct language first, then execution in simulation,
      then interoperability. Each stage is meant to be verifiable before the next begins.
    </p>

    <h2 id="status">Development status, {roadmapSnapshot.date}</h2>
    <div class="note">
      <p>
        <strong>A dated snapshot.</strong> The statuses below were read from the core repository at
        commit <a href={snapshotUrl}><code>{snapshotShort}</code></a> on {roadmapSnapshot.date}. The
        command examples and technical facts elsewhere on this site stay pinned to core
        commit <code>{CORE_COMMIT_SHORT}</code>, which they were captured and verified against.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Delivered</strong> means merged into the core repository. <strong>Approved</strong>
        means a decision is recorded; it does not authorize implementation. <strong>Pending</strong>
        and <strong>Planned</strong> mean the work has not started.
      </p>
    </div>

    <h3>Milestones</h3>
    <div class="table-scroll">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th scope="col">Stage</th>
            <th scope="col">Focus</th>
            <th scope="col">Status</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">Language 1.1 (RL-11)</th>
            <td>
              Language correctness: specification and grammar, parser, static validation, versioned IR,
              the <code>validate</code> and <code>inspect</code> commands and an installable package.
            </td>
            <td>
              <strong>Delivered</strong> as distribution {versions.distribution}
            </td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">Foundation 1 (RL-BASIC-01)</th>
            <td>
              Simulation-only execution: an executor with explicit states, preconditions evaluated
              against a declared world file, fail-closed behavior and a deterministic run record
              (<code>robolang simulate</code>).
            </td>
            <td>
              <strong>Delivered</strong>, simulation only. Signed off 2026-10-27
              (<a href={specs.foundation1Evidence}>evidence</a>)
            </td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">Language 1.2 (LANG-1)</th>
            <td>
              Conditions combined with <code>and</code>, <code>or</code> and <code>not</code>, and IR
              schema 1.1. A language version, separate from the milestones in this table.
            </td>
            <td><strong>Delivered</strong></td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">Gate 1 (RL-ARCH-02)</th>
            <td>
              Core {versions.core} message and capability contracts, and an in-process reference
              simulation in which an AI planner stub and two robot stubs exchange A2R and R2R messages
              (<code>robolang arch02</code>).
            </td>
            <td>
              <strong>Simulation only.</strong> Gate 1 passed, signed off 2026-10-27
              (<a href={specs.gate1Evidence}>evidence</a>)
            </td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">Gate 2 specification (RL-INTEROP-01)</th>
            <td>
              Independent interoperability: a participant built in a different codebase, from the
              published documents alone, works with the reference implementation.
            </td>
            <td>
              <strong>Approved</strong> 2026-10-27 (<a href={specs.gate2}>gate 2 specification</a>).
              Gate 2 itself has not passed
            </td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">
              <a href={roadmapSnapshot.interop1}>INTEROP-1</a>
            </th>
            <td>
              External-process reference self-test: the reference amr-07 runs as a separate process
              through the <code>robolang-sim-stdio</code> test binding, and all 24 reference cases
              reproduce their golden records.
            </td>
            <td><strong>Delivered</strong> 28 October 2026, simulation only</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">
              <a href={roadmapSnapshot.interop2a}>INTEROP-2a</a>
            </th>
            <td>
              Decision review of the Gate 2 specification bundle, and a traceability audit of the
              reference amr-07 against it.
            </td>
            <td>
              <strong>Completed.</strong> PR #38 merged as <code>{snapshotShort}</code> on 28 October
              2026
            </td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">Physical robots</th>
            <td>Running tasks on real hardware, through adapters and local safety controls.</td>
            <td>Not started and not authorized</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h3>What INTEROP-2a decided and found</h3>
    <div class="table-scroll">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th scope="col">Item</th>
            <th scope="col">What it is</th>
            <th scope="col">Status</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">N1–N14</th>
            <td>
              Fourteen clarifications to the Gate 2 specification, the reference simulation and
              Core {versions.core}, recorded in decision record 0011. Writing them into the
              specification text is separate work.
            </td>
            <td><strong>Approved</strong> 28 October 2026 (technical approval)</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">Traceability audit</th>
            <td>
              Every behavior of the reference amr-07 traced to the specification: 144 rows, of
              which 59 are traced, 67 ambiguous and 18 missing.
            </td>
            <td><strong>Completed</strong></td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">D1–D7</th>
            <td>
              Defects found in the reference implementation. Their correction approaches have been
              reviewed.
            </td>
            <td>Not implemented. Each needs separate authorization</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">H1, H2</th>
            <td>Refinements to how the test harness compares observations and claims.</td>
            <td>Pending. Not authorized by the decision approval</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h2 id="gate-2">Gate 2 development sequence</h2>
    <div class="note">
      <p>
        <strong>Gate 2 has not passed.</strong> No independent implementation exists yet, and Gate 2
        verification has not been run. Gate 2 tests interoperability in simulation, through a test
        binding between processes. It does not cover a real network transport, production
        authentication (signatures stay simulation-only), hardware safety or physical execution.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="table-scroll">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th scope="col">Step</th>
            <th scope="col">Work</th>
            <th scope="col">Status</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">1. INTEROP-1</th>
            <td>External-process binding and the reference self-test.</td>
            <td><strong>Delivered</strong></td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">2. INTEROP-2a</th>
            <td>Specification decisions and the traceability audit.</td>
            <td><strong>Approved and merged</strong></td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">3. Corrections</th>
            <td>
              Reference and harness corrections: silence and hold expiry, receiver-check ordering,
              the other reference defects, regression tests, and harness refinements H1 and H2.
            </td>
            <td>Pending separate authorization</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">4. INTEROP-2b</th>
            <td>
              Frozen specification bundle: implementation-independent specifications, examples,
              conformance fixtures and a SHA-256 manifest.
            </td>
            <td>Planned. Not authorized</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">5. INTEROP-3</th>
            <td>
              An independent amr-07, written in a separate codebase from the frozen bundle alone,
              behind an auditable clean-room information barrier. TypeScript is the preferred
              language.
            </td>
            <td>Planned. Not authorized</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">6. INTEROP-4</th>
            <td>
              Verification: conformance tests, interoperability scenarios, deterministic repetition,
              evidence and owner sign-off.
            </td>
            <td>Planned. Not authorized</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h2 id="beyond">Beyond Gate 2</h2>
    <div class="table-scroll">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th scope="col">Priority</th>
            <th scope="col">Workstream</th>
            <th scope="col">Planned capabilities</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td>High</td>
            <th scope="row">CORE-1</th>
            <td>Task IR reader and re-validation</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>High</td>
            <th scope="row">LANG-2 to LANG-6</th>
            <td>Branching, bounded loops, typed values, local bindings and structured data</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Next</td>
            <th scope="row">Foundation 2</th>
            <td>Goals, observations, reasoning, knowledge and bounded recovery</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Later</td>
            <th scope="row">Foundation 3</th>
            <td>Concurrency, portable skills, ROS 2 backends and heterogeneous robot support</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Later</td>
            <th scope="row">AI-native v2.0</th>
            <td>Provider-independent AI task generation, validation and benchmarking</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>
      <strong>These priorities are planning guidance.</strong> They do not authorize implementation
      and are not release commitments. Each workstream starts only with its own authorization and,
      where it changes the language, the IR or Core, its own versioned specification. None of them
      is started today.
    </p>
    <p>
      Milestones are named by their work packages. Language versions (1.1, 1.2) name the syntax
      of <code>.rob</code> files and are separate from the milestones. An earlier version of this plan
      used "v1.2" for the execution milestone; that milestone is Foundation 1 above and is unrelated
      to language 1.2. All of these are separate from the distribution version of the Python package;
      see <a href="/get-started/#versions">the version numbers</a>.
    </p>
    <p>
      The full plan, with the scope and exit criteria for each stage, is
      the <a href={specs.developmentPlan}>development plan</a> in the core repository, linked as it was at
      core commit <code>{CORE_COMMIT_SHORT}</code>; the status snapshot above is newer. The architecture
      the stages build toward is <a href={specs.rfc001}>RFC-001</a>.
    </p>

    <h2>Physical robots</h2>
    <p>
      Running RoboLang against real hardware is outside the current scope of the project. The supported
      package cannot do it: its simulations are symbolic and run in one process. The older prototype
      code in the repository has never been validated against ROS 2 or a robot. Please do not connect
      RoboLang to physical equipment.
    </p>

    <h2>Help shape it</h2>
    <p>
      The most useful input at this stage is on the language and the contracts: what is awkward to
      express, which diagnostics are unclear, what a consumer of the JSON IR or a run record needs, and
      where Core {versions.core} is ambiguous. See <a href="/contribute/">Contribute</a>.
    </p>
  </div>
</Base>
